% ============================================================================
% syllable-deletion — removing one whole syllable from a word to form a new
% word, an EIGHTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Deliberately DIFFERENT in shape from all seven prior literacy libraries:
% `word-families.adj` derives RHYMING, `syllable-count.adj` recalls a
% SYLLABLE COUNT, `onset-rime.adj` recalls an ONSET/RIME split,
% `initial-sound.adj` recalls a BEGINNING sound, `phoneme-substitution.adj`
% recalls the new word formed by swapping one SOUND, `phoneme-deletion.adj`
% recalls the new word formed by removing one SOUND, and
% `syllable-substitution.adj` recalls the position changed when one whole
% SYLLABLE is swapped. This library recalls what happens when you DELETE
% (remove) one whole SYLLABLE from a word — the syllable-level analogue of
% `phoneme-deletion.adj`, the same way `syllable-substitution.adj` is the
% syllable-level analogue of `phoneme-substitution.adj` — grounding the same
% phonemic/phonological-awareness continuum's deletion skill at the coarser
% syllable grain, as a pure lookup:
% `syllable_deletion(original_word, removed_syllable, new_word)`. Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? syllable_deletion(pencil, cil, $New)   % pen
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table shaped exactly
% like `phoneme-deletion.adj` and `onset-rime.adj`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `syllable_deletion(original_word, removed_syllable,
% new_word)` carrying the Reading Rockets citation, so the lookup above is
% the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the new word WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any deletion
% not the one the cited page walks through. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind the new word and recall which word it came from and which syllable
% was removed:
%
%     ? syllable_deletion($Orig, $Syllable, pen)   % pencil, cil
%
% The one deletion this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     original_word   removed_syllable   new_word
%     -------------   ----------------   --------
%     pencil          cil                pen
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`, `phoneme-substitution.adj`, `phoneme-deletion.adj`,
% and `syllable-substitution.adj` already cite (its "Deleting syllables"
% section, distinct from the "Deleting Sounds" section `phoneme-deletion.adj`
% already draws from) — WebFetch-verified THREE separate times for
% consistency before writing, all byte-identical. The exact spans, composed
% together the same way `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s row already is (two
% distinct clean-prose fragments from the same cited section, each stating
% one fact — deliberately NOT drawing from the section's bracketed
% stage-direction text or its generic closing instruction, "Repeat these
% steps with as many as 15 two-syllable words," which names no further
% concrete word and so grounds nothing beyond this one worked example):
%
%   "The word is: 'pencil'."
%     (states the original word: pencil)
%   "I take away 'cil' [remove the card]. 'Pen' is left [touch remaining
%    card]."
%     (states which syllable was removed and what remains: cil -> pen)
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s,
% `syllable-substitution.adj`'s, and `syllable-count.adj`'s sibling sections
% of this page already earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE
% deletion the cited page walks through step by step — honestly narrow
% (mirroring `phoneme-deletion.adj`'s and `syllable-substitution.adj`'s
% precedent) rather than inventing a deletion the source does not
% demonstrate. A future slice can add a second deletion once a comparably
% clean citation is found. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table syllable_deletion {
    columns original_word, removed_syllable, new_word

    row (pencil, cil, pen)

    source "The word is: 'pencil'. I take away 'cil'. 'Pen' is left."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
